messages:
kai: Heads-up — Acme's Private VPC hit the GPU ceiling this morning during their 09:00 batch window. Customers saw a bunch of 429s and some timeouts. Need a short, actionable note we can give them for immediate client-side throttling + graceful degradation.

maya-cs: Oof. Their batch jobs spike a lot. Do we have a one-pager we can send or should we give code samples?

mira: Short checklist first: (1) enforce a request-level RPS cap, (2) enforce a token/sec cap, (3) implement jittered exponential backoff on 429/503, (4) provide a lightweight local queue with hard limits and drop/route to fallback when queue full.

sam: Agree. Add: prefer rejecting early vs letting sockets pile up. We should recommend returning 429 + Retry-After header from the customer-side gateway, and include a small explanatory JSON body so the client can decide: e.g., {"error":"capacity_limit","retry_after":30,"recommended_action":"reduce_batch_or_switch_model"}.

jess: From oncall: DO NOT advise unbounded server-side queues. If a customer adds a big in-process queue in front of the VPC endpoint we just translate latency to memory pressure. Recommend a bounded queue (size ~ 2-5x concurrency), with hard-drop policy for excess.

mira: Example token-bucket pseudo in JS for clients (small snippet we can paste into CS note):

```js
class TokenBucket {
  constructor(rate, burst) {
    this.rate = rate; // tokens per sec
    this.burst = burst;
    this.tokens = burst;
    this.last = Date.now();
  }
  take(n=1) {
    const now = Date.now();
    this.tokens = Math.min(this.burst, this.tokens + (now - this.last)/1000 * this.rate);
    this.last = now;
    if (this.tokens >= n) { this.tokens -= n; return true; }
    return false;
  }
}
// usage: tokens ~ estimated tokens per request; if take() fails -> backoff
```

sam: And combine with jittered backoff on failures: 100-200ms base + full jitter up to exponential cap (e.g., 10s). Always add small randomized jitter to avoid thundering herd.

maya-cs: What about graceful degrade options we can tell customers to implement quickly?

mira: Two quick patterns: (A) model fallback: if primary model returns 429 or latency > threshold, route to a cheaper quantized variant or lower max_tokens call. (B) trimmed requests: proactively reduce max_tokens for non-critical calls during peak windows. Both are quick server-side switches.

jess: Also give them headers to expose from their gateway so we can debug: x-client-queue-size, x-client-retries, x-forwarded-region, and include a correlation id. That helps us correlate to infra saturation.

eng-oncall-bot: Incident note: private-vpc.acme — capacity-ceiling detected 1729054321, auto-alert created INC-2024-3912.

kai: Good. I want a concise CS-friendly template. Short bullet list + two copy-paste snippets (token-bucket + HTTP error example). Keep it <200 words.

maya-cs: Draft message for customers: 

"We observed transient capacity limits in Private VPC deployments during your peak batch window. To reduce failed requests and improve reliability, we recommend: enforce a per-connection request rate limit, cap tokens/sec, implement jittered exponential backoff on 429/503, use a small bounded ingress queue with hard-drop, and add an automatic fallback to a cheaper model or lower max_tokens. Return 429 with a Retry-After header for rejects and include a short JSON body indicating retry timing and suggested mitigation. Consider shifting non-urgent batch jobs to off-peak windows or upgrading to reserved capacity for guaranteed throughput."

sam: HTTP error example we can paste: 

```http
HTTP/1.1 429 Too Many Requests
Content-Type: application/json
Retry-After: 30
X-Rate-Limit-Reason: capacity_ceiling

{
  "error": "capacity_limit",
  "retry_after_seconds": 30,
  "suggested_action": "reduce_batch_size_or_use_fallback_model"
}
```

mira: Also include an ops tip: if they can, expose separate rate pools for interactive vs batch traffic and throttle batch first when capacity tightens. Batch can be rate-limited to low-priority windows.

jess: One more thing — logging: ask them to emit a 1-line sample when they drop a request (include reason, queue_pos if any, and estimated wait). That helps post-mortem.

kai: Perfect. I'll send this with a quick TODO checklist + the two snippets. Add a note offering a 30-min call to walk them through implementing the token bucket and fallback. Thanks all. :thumbsup:
